        "thread_id": "TH2",
        "thread_goal": "Define standards for journal entries and supporting documentation.",
        "messages": [
          {
            "msg_id": "M15",
            "speaker_id": 457591,
            "reply_to": null,
            "text": "Hi team, as per T2 and T5, I've drafted the standard journal entry templates and the documentation standards. Please see the proposal in A4. The goal is to improve consistency and auditability. I've also included a draft of the peer review checklist A7 for high-risk entries as part of T7.",
            "mentions": [
              81248,
              16157,
              375245,
              523876
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "T2",
              "T5",
              "T7",
              "A4",
              "A7"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M16",
            "speaker_id": 523876,
            "reply_to": "M15",
            "text": "This is excellent, very clear. The peer review checklist in A7 is a great addition. For the criteria of 'high-risk', is it just based on a monetary threshold or also complexity?",
            "mentions": [
              457591
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A7"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M17",
            "speaker_id": 457591,
            "reply_to": "M16",
            "text": "Good question. I've proposed it's a combination of both: any entry over a certain value, plus non-standard entries like manual revenue adjustments or provisions, regardless of value. We can refine the list.",
            "mentions": [
              523876
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M18",
            "speaker_id": 375245,
            "reply_to": "M15",
            "text": "Looking at A4, for the naming convention, can we just use the date and our initials? Like '20231025_BK'. It seems simpler.",
            "mentions": [],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A4"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M19",
            "speaker_id": 16157,
            "reply_to": "M18",
            "text": "Using just initials could cause issues if we have multiple bookkeepers with the same initials, and it doesn't describe the entry. The proposed format in A4 (e.g., 'YYYYMMDD_Accrual_VendorName_JE#') is better for traceability during an audit. It allows anyone to understand the file's content without opening it.",
            "mentions": [
              375245
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [
              "A4"
            ],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": [
              {
                "emoji": "point_up",
                "count": 2
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M20",
            "speaker_id": 375245,
            "reply_to": "M19",
            "text": "Oh, I see. That makes sense.",
            "mentions": [
              16157
            ],
            "artifact_refs": [],
            "decision_refs": [],
            "reactions": []
          },
          {
            "msg_id": "M21",
            "speaker_id": 81248,
            "reply_to": "M15",
            "text": "This looks very thorough. Is the team generally happy with these proposals for the templates and the documentation rules?",
